  
1886 Problem:-
Given two n x n binary matrices mat and target, return true if it is possible to make mat equal to target by rotating mat in 90-degree increments, or false otherwise.

Example 1:
Input: mat = [[0,1],[1,0]], target = [[1,0],[0,1]]
Output: true
Explanation: We can rotate mat 90 degrees clockwise to make mat equal target.

Example 2:
Input: mat = [[0,1],[1,1]], target = [[1,0],[0,1]]
Output: false
Explanation: It is impossible to make mat equal to target by rotating mat.

Example 3:
Input: mat = [[0,0,0],[0,1,0],[1,1,1]], target = [[1,1,1],[0,1,0],[0,0,0]]
Output: true
Explanation: We can rotate mat 90 degrees clockwise two times to make mat equal target.
 
Constraints:-
n == mat.length == target.length
n == mat[i].length == target[i].length
1 <= n <= 10
mat[i][j] and target[i][j] are either 0 or 1.

=================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
🧠 Intuition
A square matrix has only 4 possible orientations when rotated in 90° increments:
0° (original)
90°
180°
270°
Instead of trying complex transformations, we can simply:
Compare the matrix with target
Rotate the matrix
Repeat up to 4 times
If any rotation matches → return true.

🚀 Approach
Step 1: Rotation Logic (90° Clockwise)
We rotate the matrix using:
Transpose → swap across diagonal
Reverse rows → complete rotation
Step 2: Try All Rotations
Loop 4 times
After each rotation, compare with target

🔍 Formula Insight
The 90° clockwise rotation follows this transformation:
👉 new[j][n - i - 1] = mat[i][j]

🧠 What this means
Row index i becomes column index j
Column index j becomes reversed row position n - i - 1
👉 In simple words:
"Row becomes column, and column gets reversed"

⏱️ Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n²)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Code:-
class Solution {
    public void rotate(int[][] mat) {
        int n = mat.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int temp = mat[i][j];
                mat[i][j] = mat[j][i];
                mat[j][i] = temp;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n / 2; j++) {
                int temp = mat[i][j];
                mat[i][j] = mat[i][n - j - 1];
                mat[i][n - j - 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }

    public boolean findRotation(int[][] mat, int[][] target) {
        for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
            if (isEqual(mat, target)) return true;
            rotate(mat);
        }
        return false;
    }

    private boolean isEqual(int[][] a, int[][] b) {
        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < a[0].length; j++) {
                if (a[i][j] != b[i][j]) return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
